ould be you know what happens  when we're getting more  questioning and abstraction going on  so there's there's a lot to be said  about the activity of the human mind and  we could also say  not just a single human mind human minds  in relation to each other  that constitutes philosophizing and then  we can also talk about  philosophy as something like a  vocation that some people  take as their orienting principle their  life's work  uh as their way of life  and there is indeed something that is  called philosophy as a way of life that  has become  recognized since the you know midpoint  of the 20th century  onward associated with that great phrase  philosophy is a way of life with pure  odd dull but  existing throughout the ages and  and you know in fact a lot of ancient  and medieval  and early modern philosophy was  philosophy as a way of of life and  all four of these and this is not an ex  you know  entirely comprehensive mapping here but  all four of these  are interconnected with each other and  so when people  are debating or arguing with each other  well that's not philosophy and no this  is philosophy is this the best  philosophy  one person might be thinking in terms of  philosophy as a discipline we don't  study that here in our philosophy  department  you can say well why not it's  philosophical  literature it could lead to a certain  way of living you may be using the same  mental activities capacities  dispositions to do it as with other  things that we call  philosophy just doesn't happen to be  taught here instead it gets taught you  know like for example alexis de  tocqueville  who's clearly a philosopher gets taught  in political science departments more  often than in  philosophy departments and you know we  could also talk about you know  too exclusive a focus on is it published  or not  well you know socrates didn't publish a  single thing people published things  about him including plato  and xenophon but also eskinese and a few  other people  writing socratic dialogues you know  is is that important that somebody has  books articles blog posts  whatever to their account somebody might  focus entirely  on philosophy as an activity and say oh  you know i'm not a fuddy-duddy  like those people in the philosophy  department or the people who are  beholden enslaved to the literature of  philosophy i'm gonna  make new paths for myself unfortunately  what you find out is  usually when somebody claims to be doing  that  you look in the literature and  somebody's already done what they're  doing including that very same i'm  breaking with tradition  right and all of these you know kind of  come together  and reinforce each other it could be  useful  to think about you know a prototypical  set of philosophers and think about how  they  figure into this so let's take socrates  for example right  socrates very very clearly  thinks that philosophy is not just an  activity  and could become a discipline although  it's not really a discipline in his time  but he you know he sketches out at least  according to plato  some some modes in which that could take  place but he  definitely practiced philosophy as a way  of life  and as a vocation to the point that it  got him killed where he tells them  hey if you're going to tell me to stop  philosophizing or  be executed go ahead and execute me  right  philosophy is literature does kind of  exist at the time that socrates  is in you know teaching all of his  his uh friends and students you know he  mentions annex agaris  buying his scrolls reading through them  and saying well this is  this isn't true you know so you know  we also have an entire literature  generated  around this guy socrates right i  mentioned platonic dialogues  xenophon's dialogues there's all sorts  of other stories  told about him in which he appears as  kind of the prototypical  philosopher and by observing these we're  supposed to learn  what philosophy as activity consists in  and we can say that for the later  discipline of philosophy  philosophies you find it in academic  departments and textbooks and  things like that socrates is at least  referenced if not always understood  um you know a lot of people will say oh  he's he's the guy who said  i i'm wiser than everyone else because i  know that i don't know anything and  that's that's the tip of the iceberg  with socrates but  you will find you know introductory text  talking about  his dictum the unexamined life is not  worth living and then moving on to other  things  what about another philosopher let's  think about rene descartes  considered to be one of the fathers not  only of modern philosophy but of  modernity  in general descartes himself  did write things he generates a  literature  um not a vast literature as a matter of  fact  harry fronkford a great 20th century  philosopher  said that one reason he became a  descartes scholar was because when he  was looking at the body of literature  that he would have to master he saw that  the cartesian corpus was  rather small and being inclined towards  laziness he decided i'll become a  descartes scholar  rather than you know taking on the  corpus of somebody who had much more  writings he may have been doing some  false humility  at that time but um descartes certainly  does generate a literature that we still  read today  he's also responding to the literature  of his time  the literature that he's engaged in  think about the  meditations on first philosophy what  follows that  objections and replies including from  some  very famous philosophers in their own  respect like thomas hobbs or pierre  gascendy  both of whom descartes didn't really get  along with right  descartes is displaying in that  literature a new way  of carrying out philosophical  activity he also is looking at the  university system of his time  and saying philosophy as a discipline  is in bad shape you know we need to  reform it and we reform it  by thinking about the activity and what  it would require you know moving away  for example from  you know the thinking about things in  terms of the body and into the the mind  freed from the body which we can only do  through  discipline this is where philosophy as a  way of life comes in  descartes sketches out  a way of living that was attractive to  many  people for example in the the play  cyrano de bergerac  right we find a point where cyrano  actually  says i'm going to read descartes why  because descartes was viewed as  providing  some path forward that one could  incorporate into one's life  and let's think about you know other  people maybe down the line we could we  could put them into this matrix and say  where do they fit in with us a few other  points i'd like to make that i think  could be helpful for wrapping your head  around this  so philosophy is a discipline it's been  around for a long time  there there's never been some absolutely  agreed upon standards or cannon  or motive organization and at the same  time  it's not you know well anything goes  in order to be academic philosophy  traditionally there has to be somebody  who's actually in the position of  a teacher a professor and they're  selected by  others and you could think of it as a  guild or a cast now in in recent times  of course  it's become professionalized but in  in a lot of different ways so what  counts as  you know meeting the requirements of the  discipline in  one college might not be the same thing  as in another college and so we have  this incredibly diverse  discipline going on that we  call philosophy with a lot of different  things under that  heading and it's often part of an  educational structure  in the middle ages philosophy  was what the in the west what the seven  liberal arts flowed into  and then philosophy was a stopping point  before you did your real degree  in law medicine or theology but there  were people who just practiced  philosophy as such in the discipline  just like there were people who  specialized in rhetoric  and now you know we have people who  get their masters or phds generally phd  if you want to teach because there's  there's so many people on the market  and they try to get jobs within the  university system maybe they're  successful maybe they're not  and then they contribute to the ongoing  development of that discipline a lot of  which is  you know teaching new generations in in  place  philosophy of literature vast amounts  right going all the way back into  antiquity  and this also includes not just the  original  language literature but also  translations alexander of aphrodisias  for example a great aristotelian  commentator who i'm a big fan of  most of his stuff was not translated  until recent decades into english so it  was largely  inaccessible the person he was  commenting on aristotle of course  translations have existed for you know a  very long time haven't they  more than a millennium there's a  vast philosophical literature out there  you can see this by going to libraries  or bookstores or going online  much of it is carried out in a variety  of different  genres ranging from poetry and drama  all the way to very abstract often  mind-numbingly sterile academic articles  and journals and everything  in between there is no absolute  agreement on where the boundaries of  philosophical literature lie  philosophy is an activity oftentimes  this can be tied in with  understandings of methodology or  starting points or the ways in which  we're supposed to be engaging in  philosophy  and it includes as i mentioned before  not just what you go off and do in your  room by yourself  but also what you do in relation to  other philosophers and  that may include going to the literature  and saying well what does  what does rene descartes what does  benedict spinoza what does  david hume what does mary wallstonecraft  have to talk to tell me  to teach me to throw in my face that i  need to then  react to and respond to and think  through you know partially accept  partially reject  or buy in wholesale uh finally  philosophy is a vocation or as a way of  life this is something  that we do see people practicing  in large part outside of academia  we live in an extraordinary time in  which the internet  has opened up access to  hundreds of thousands maybe millions of  people to engage in philosophy as a way  of life outside  of the academic discipline but informed  by  the literature carrying out the activity  and occasionally coinciding with  philosophy as a discipline  so these are all important aspects of  philosophy  when somebody says philosophy is this  philosophy does this this person's a  philosopher this person isn't  you want to ask well what sense of  philosophy  do they have in mind and this might be  helpful for you  you

